    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Rocky ridges and quartzite cliffs on the western side of Bull Run Mountain once guided slaves fleeing via the Underground Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pecva.org/land-conservation/history-at-the-top-of-bull-run-mountain/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The mountainside of Bull Run Mountain Estates features historic ruins, including the Chapman-Beverly Mill built in 1742.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pecva.org/land-conservation/history-at-the-top-of-bull-run-mountain/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Bull Run Mountains Natural Preserve Area protects 2,500 acres of the range for its natural and historic resources.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pecva.org/land-conservation/history-at-the-top-of-bull-run-mountain/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bull Run Mountain has a history of free Black and poor White families who once lived among the rocks and thin soils.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pecva.org/land-conservation/history-at-the-top-of-bull-run-mountain/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Bull Run Mountains stretch 15 miles from the village of Aldie in Loudoun County south to New Baltimore in Fauquier County.",
